- F-73 **The compiled checker infers a generic's static arguments with coercion: a type parameter that is the whole type of each parameter it occurs in, as `T` in `pick[\T\](x: T, y: T)`, takes the narrowest type that every argument converts into and its bound permits.** It looks for that type among the arguments' static types, their coercion targets and its bound, the bound only when it is a trait type other than `Object` or `Any`. It builds P-74 and P-49. Walk applies the same rule to the arguments' run-time types (F-137). So the two paths take the same type where each argument's static type is its run-time type, as in microGPT, and may differ where a static type is wider than its value (row 509). A coerced declaration ranks below one that needs no coercion. A tie among coerced declarations is refused, "Ambiguous coercion", outside three narrow cases. At a tie that differs only where the call passes a numeral, the checker reads the numeral as `ZZ32`, or as `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit, which builds P-173. The coercion attempt refuses a call with an untyped function argument (row 507), and a call with more than 64 combinations of the types that it chooses for the lone type parameters (F-137), a parameter that numerals alone fix counting as one (row 506). Gated by `compiler_tests/NatLitArgChecker`, `CoercionGenericFnCompiledRungC` and `InferCoercionShapes`, and the refusal of a tie by `XXXInferAmbiguousCoercion`. Detail, with the three cases: `compile-ladder/rung-inference-checker/REPORT.md` section 3.2.
  by: the clarity pass; note: F-55's conclusion merged in, with row 509's limit; moved out: "a type parameter that numerals alone fix, with no expected type, takes the narrowest default that holds them all", held by F-342; the repeated "the report's section 3.2"

- F-78 **The compiled checker instantiates a type parameter that nothing at a call fixes at the intersection of its upper bounds, never `BottomType`, and tries a call by subtyping without the expected type first.** It builds P-48 and P-49. With an expected type, the attempts run in this order: subtyping without it, kept when the result converts to it; subtyping with it; coercion with it; coercion without it. P-49 decides the first and the third. A worker chose the second and the fourth (D4 of `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/decision-record.md`). The second type-checks a call whose untyped function argument only the expected type fixes, which the coercion attempt refuses (row 507). An argument of intersection type fixes a parameter at the whole intersection (row 541). The run time has no class for an intersection type, so an instance at one, from such an argument or from an unfixed parameter's bounds, dies as it loads (row 559). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferResultOnly*` and `InferContextKeepsFit`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 7.
  by: the clarity pass; note: the stale pointer Functionals.scala:962-966 dropped: the attempt list is now `val attempts` at Functionals.scala:1027-1031, read 2026-11-24, and agrees with the order stated; "an agent's choice, the instance-bound rung's" said as a worker's choice with its decision record; row 559's scope from the ledger (every instance at an intersection type, by four routes)

- F-87 **The compiled checker gives a call its expected type in many contexts, among them a typed declaration's right-hand side, a clause of an `if` without `else` (the type `()`), a block's last expression after local declarations, a `typecase` clause, a juxtaposition, an operator repeated between three or more operands, a `label` body and each value that an `exit` gives the `label`, and another call's argument, by a retry of the enclosing call (F-221).** The inference chapter's paragraph "The expected type" (`Specification/basic/inference.tex`, "The Static Arguments of a Call") lists every context, the same set that the checker gives. In a juxtaposition or a repeated operator, the expression's own expected type goes to the call that it desugars to. The `label` contexts build P-50. Gated by `compiler_tests/InferResultOnly*` and `Infer*Context`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-contexts/REPORT.md` section 1.
  by: the clarity pass; note: the title stated as the current list, not against "the contexts that it already had"; "The expected type" is an \emph paragraph label at inference.tex:129, inside the cited section; the chapter's list and the checker's agree (rung-checker-contexts/REPORT.md sections 1 and 10); moved out: the retry's sentence with P-51 (F-221 holds it); the pointers to F-226 (renaming an inherited method's static parameters) and F-70 (a written static argument against its bound), neither about the expected type; InferResultOnlyLabelBody by name (the pattern covers it)

- F-221 **The compiled checker retries a call written as another call's argument against each enclosing declaration's parameter type, where the enclosing call is not accepted otherwise.** It builds P-51 under P-49, so a declaration that accepts the call as written is never passed over. A reduction is a call of `__bigOperator` with the big operator as an argument. So where a plain-bounded big operator's bound does not fit, the retry gives it the reduction's expected type; one whose bound names its parameter takes that type, else its body's (F-364). An operator chain `a OPLUS b OPLUS c` that a multifix declaration of `OPLUS`, one over three or more operands, accepts only through the retry runs that declaration, not the binary one. Walk gives such an argument no expected type (row 705). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferCallArgument*` and `InferBigOperatorExpectedType`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-argument-context/REPORT.md` sections 1, 5 and 8, and its `SKEPTIC.md` finding 5.
  by: the clarity pass; note: the referent of "so" made explicit (the retry gives the big operator the expected type); the plain-bound condition from F-59 ("takes its bound, or the reduction's expected type where the bound does not fit"); "now runs it" said in the present; SKEPTIC.md is rung-argument-context/SKEPTIC.md

- F-59 **`SUM` and `PROD` take their identity, zero or one, from their static argument; where none is written, the compiled checker infers it (F-364).** It builds P-78 with a witness `typecase`: a `typecase` over a `() -> T` function, with one clause for each number type, which picks the identity of the type `T`. A sum without generators, `SUM e` over a collection `e` or `x DOT y`, writes no static argument (P-78). C4, microGPT's flat program, writes none in its one clause-form sum, the body of a top-level function declared to return `ZZ32`, so both paths sum at `ZZ32` (walk by F-228). The team's tests and the interpreter's library write it (F-285). Gated by `compiler_tests/WitnessIdentityRungG`. Detail: `reviews/sum-replacement-judgement.md` section 5, Table 2, measured before P-48 and F-364.
  by: the clarity pass; note: kept to the library's identity rule and C4's sum; moved out, each to its owner: the checker's instance from the expected type else the body's type for SUM, PROD, BIG MAX and BIG MIN (F-364); a plain-bounded big operator at its bound or the expected type (F-221, F-364); walk's instance by declared type, else join, else open (F-228, F-143); the sum written out, ∑[j <- 0#i] matCount(j) (explorations/run-c4/src/MicroGptFlat.fss:38); the tests InferBigOperatorUnwritten and InferBigOperatorSelfBound (F-364); "Table 2" is a bold line, not a heading, in section 5; "a sum without generators" is P-78's wording

- F-364 **The compiled checker instantiates a reduction that writes no static argument by its expected type, else by the type of its body, where the call as written is not accepted; a reduction that is accepted as written is unchanged.** The checker first checks the call as written, which it can where the big operator alone has an instance: a plain bound, or no static parameter. Where that is not accepted, or its result does not convert to the expected type, the checker writes the static argument as the expected type, then as the body's type, each only where it meets the big operator's bound. It keeps the first form that is accepted and converts. The elements are converted to the instance. A call's argument has no expected type where the call is accepted without one (P-51), so a reduction there runs at its body's type. So `SUM`, `PROD`, `BIG MIN` and `BIG MAX` need no written static argument for the checker. At a method's return type, a field, a top-level variable and an assignment, the two paths give such a reduction different instances (row 1008), so a program for both paths writes the argument there. A big operator with two or more static parameters is refused (row 1007). Where the bound names its parameter, a type error in the reduction's body is reported as the big operator's refusal (row 1015). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferBigOperatorSelfBound`, and guarded by `InferBigOperatorUnwritten`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-static-argument-inferred/REPORT.md`, "Step 1, row 731: the reduction".
  by: the clarity pass; note: the 90-word sentence split; "now written so" was ambiguous: MicroGptFlat.fss:38 writes no static argument (read 2026-11-24); moved to the report: reductionApplication in scala_src/typechecker/impls/Functionals.scala; the measurement observed once, in copies with the argument removed, that the library's two BIG MIN sites and its list comprehension and C4's sum give no error ("5. Old against new"); the library and C4 sentence (F-59, F-285 point here); the guard InferBigOperatorExpectedType (F-221 names it); the section "7. The stages and the checker's time"

- F-365 **The compiled checker instantiates a reference to a generic function by the arrow type that receives it, and refuses one that the arrow type leaves open.** This holds for a name with one declaration, which has static parameters, referenced without static arguments where an arrow type is expected; as a call's argument, it is typed again in the retry (F-221). A static parameter that the arrow type's domain does not fix, such as one that only the return type mentions, is refused with a message that names it. An arrow type that carries its own static parameters is a subtype of no arrow type and coerces to nothing. The function's own static parameters count as unbound also inside a generic declaration whose static parameter has the same name. The memo of normalized arrow types keys an arrow type by its static parameters too, so a generic function's type and an instance written alike stay apart. Refused: a name with several declarations of which one is generic (row 1009), and the applied value of an untyped binding such as `g = count`. Stopped, 'F is not in the kind env': such a binding never applied (row 1010). Accepted, then dead at run time: a reference where the expected type is not an arrow type, as at a parameter of type `Any` (row 1012), and a generic trait's functional method passed as a value inside the trait (row 1013). No test compiles the model's row lift, `rows(rmsn, x)`, whose generic callee's other argument fixes the sizes, so whether the checker accepts it is unmeasured. Gated by `compiler_tests/*GenericFunctionAsValue*Compiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-static-argument-inferred/REPORT.md`, "Step 2, row 758: the reference to a generic function".
  by: the clarity pass; note: "the test" was GenericFunctionAsValueCompiled, whose useSized(half) passes a sized generic function to a callee that is not generic (read 2026-11-24); the outcome of rows 1009 and 1010 named from the ledger; moved to the report (step 2): instantiatedReference and retypable in Functionals.scala; the static arguments that make the instance a subtype of the arrow type; the refusal's text for pick; the files TypeAnalyzer.scala, CoercionOracle.scala:292-293, carriesOwnStaticParams; the section "5. Old against new"; four tests by name

- F-367 **Under walk, a generic function is a value, received at the arrow type of its parameter or variable.** As a value, a function with static parameters has a type of its own, below `Any` and below each arrow type at which it can be received. A parameter, a local variable declared with an arrow type, and a variable of arrow type at an assignment, a mutable top-level variable among them, receive the instance that a direct call with arguments of the arrow's domain types runs. A generic callee's static parameters are fixed by its other arguments first, so the row lift `rows(rmsn, x)` runs with a sized `rmsn`. At an arrow type that leaves a static parameter open, at `Any`, at a type parameter and at an untyped place, the value stays generic and each application infers its static arguments, where the specification makes it a static error (row 1021, a design limit of walk). Of these, the compiled checker refuses the open arrow type and the untyped place, and accepts `Any`, whose compiled run dies (row 1012). An overloaded name's value keeps its generic declarations, so `pick(twice)`, for an overloaded `twice` with a generic declaration, runs the `pick` declared over an arrow type, not `pick(x: Any)`; no test pins this. Defects: an immutable top-level variable keeps the generic value (row 1019); a generic function passed at an arrow type over the callee's own type parameter is refused where another argument fixes that parameter by its run-time type, as `appT(half, v)` (row 1018); a function declared in a block with static parameters of its own does not run, called or passed (row 1020). Gated by `tests/GenericFunctionAsValueWalk`, `GenericFunctionRowLiftWalk` and `GenericFunctionAssignedWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-generic-function-value/REPORT.md`, "The rule as built".
  by: the clarity pass; note: corrected from the ledger: the checker refuses the open arrow type and the untyped place (row 1021, "The checker refuses both") but accepts a reference at Any (row 1012, F-365), where the old text said it refuses all those forms; row 1021 is a design limit of walk against the specification's static error; row 1018 restated from the ledger; "Not built:" replaced by "Defects:"; moved to the report: FTypeGenericArrow.java, Fcn.java received, EvaluatorBase.java instanceReceivedAt, OverloadedFunction.java:3067; a constructor's parameter among the receiving places; the section "Runs to report, not tests"

- F-141 **Under `walk`, a type parameter that nothing at a call fixes takes its declared bound, and a lone one (F-137) with no narrowest candidate takes its bound where every argument is below it, else the arguments' join.** It builds P-48. A type parameter that the parameter types mention only in function domains, an odd number of arrows deep and never inside a static argument, as `T` in `g: T -> ZZ32`, is bounded by the arguments only from above; with nothing below, it takes the meet of its declared bound and those bounds. An F-bounded parameter stays open (F-143). A worker chose two more rules as defaults (D3 and D5 of `compile-ladder/rung-walk-instance/decision-record.md`): a parameter that only another fixed parameter's bound mentions takes that parameter's instance, so `ap[\T, A extends T\](a: A)` gives `T` the type of `a` (D3, row 592); and a lone parameter whose bound mentions a static parameter has its arguments' supertypes among its candidates, so it takes their narrowest common supertype, never its bound (D5). Defects, each an expected failure: two bounds that walk cannot meet (row 591); two upper bounds from two function arguments that walk cannot meet, which fail the call (row 616); `Bottom` kept for a bound only from above that mentions another static parameter (row 612), and for a parameter that only an untyped function expression's result fixes (row 587); a generic passed over at dispatch where its arguments have several minimal common supertypes (row 589), whose repair would change which declaration runs. Gated by `tests/Infer*Walk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-instance/REPORT.md` section 2; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-meet/REPORT.md` section 1.
  by: the clarity pass; note: moved out: F-137's definition of a lone parameter (a pointer now); the rule for a reduction or comprehension that writes no static argument, held by F-228; the status words "That rule is the coordinator's call" (on F-228's rule) and, for row 589, "the curator's to decide"; the detail rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md section 1 (F-228's rule) and decision-record.md section 2 (named by its D3 and D5); the D3 and D5 rules said as a worker's defaults, each tagged

